          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which three features distinguish HIV-related skin disease from the same conditio</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which three features distinguish HIV-related skin disease from the same conditions in immunocompetent individuals?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Deeper, nodular, and haemorrhagic</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These terms describe certain individual lesion types (e.g., Kaposi sarcoma) but do not capture the broad pattern distinguishing HIV skin disease. The key triad is extensive, atypical, and refractory.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Extensive, atypical, and refractory</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. HIV-related dermatoses are more widespread in distribution, more unusual in morphology or behaviour, and more poorly responsive to standard treatment — these three red flags should prompt HIV testing.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Unilateral, vesicular, and recurrent</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While herpes zoster in HIV may be multidermatomal or recurrent, this combination does not define the general pattern. The hallmarks are extensive, atypical, and refractory.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pigmented, scarring, and pruritic</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation and pruritus occur in conditions like PPE, but they are not the distinguishing triad. The three hallmarks are extensive distribution, atypical morphology, and refractory course.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The three features that should raise HIV suspicion are that skin lesions are more extensive (widespread rather than localised), more atypical (unusual morphology or distribution), and more refractory (poorly responsive to standard therapy)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Remembering 'extensive, atypical, and refractory' is the key clinical heuristic for suspecting HIV when a common skin condition behaves unusually."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
