          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An HIV-positive patient with a CD4 count of 80 presents with violaceous nodular </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An HIV-positive patient with a CD4 count of 80 presents with violaceous nodular lesions. Which investigation is definitive for distinguishing Kaposi sarcoma from bacillary angiomatosis and pyogenic granuloma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>KOH mount of skin scrapings</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>KOH mounts identify fungal elements in dermatophyte or candidal infections. They cannot differentiate between vascular lesions such as KS, bacillary angiomatosis, and pyogenic granuloma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CD4 count and viral load</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CD4 count and viral load assess overall immune status and guide ART decisions, but they cannot distinguish between different vascular lesions. Histopathological examination is required.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Skin biopsy with histopathology</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Although KS, bacillary angiomatosis, and pyogenic granuloma can appear clinically similar, their histopathological features are distinct — making biopsy the definitive diagnostic tool.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tzanck smear from the base of the lesion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A Tzanck smear detects multinucleated giant cells in herpetic vesicular infections. It has no role in differentiating vascular or neoplastic lesions at low CD4 counts.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Kaposi sarcoma, bacillary angiomatosis, and pyogenic granuloma can all present as violaceous vascular-appearing lesions at low CD4 counts. Skin biopsy with histopathology is the definitive way to separate them — critically important because bacillary angiomatosis is curable with antibiotics."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Skin biopsy is essential when violaceous vascular lesions appear at low CD4 counts, because bacillary angiomatosis — unlike KS — is an antibiotic-curable bacterial infection that must not be missed."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
