          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An HIV-positive patient on nevirapine develops painful skin with mucosal erosion</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An HIV-positive patient on nevirapine develops painful skin with mucosal erosions, a positive Nikolsky sign, and epidermal detachment involving 18% of body surface area. How is this classified on the SJS–TEN spectrum?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Stevens-Johnson syndrome (&lt; 10% BSA detachment)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>SJS is defined by less than 10% BSA of epidermal detachment. At 18% BSA, this patient has crossed into the overlap zone, indicating a more severe reaction requiring urgent intervention.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>DRESS presents with rash, fever, facial oedema, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia, and organ involvement — but its hallmark is NOT epidermal detachment with a positive Nikolsky sign. The BSA-based grading applies to the SJS–TEN spectrum specifically.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>SJS–TEN overlap (10–30% BSA detachment)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. With 18% BSA of epidermal detachment, this falls in the SJS–TEN overlap zone (10–30%). This classification carries a worse prognosis than SJS and demands immediate nevirapine cessation and urgent supportive care.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Toxic epidermal necrolysis (&gt; 30% BSA detachment)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>TEN is defined by more than 30% BSA of epidermal detachment. At 18%, this patient is in the SJS–TEN overlap zone (10–30%), though progression to TEN remains a risk without prompt drug cessation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The SJS–TEN spectrum is classified by BSA of epidermal detachment: SJS (< 10%), SJS–TEN overlap (10–30%), and TEN (> 30%). At 18% BSA, this patient falls in the overlap zone — a severe reaction requiring immediate nevirapine cessation and urgent supportive care."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Accurate BSA-based classification of epidermal detachment is critical — it directly determines prognosis and the urgency of supportive care in drug-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
